# Environment for the Raid Clash Tracker. Copy this file to .env.local (which is
# gitignored) and fill in your own values. Never commit a real connection string —
# it contains your database password.

# The one and only database: a Supabase Postgres project, reached over its POOLED
# connection on port 6543. Shape only — replace every <ANGLE-BRACKET> placeholder:
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<PROJECT-REF>:<YOUR-DB-PASSWORD>@<PREFIX>-<REGION>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# Don't assemble the host by hand. The pooler hostname is NOT just your region —
# it carries a platform prefix in front of it (`aws-0-<region>`, `aws-1-<region>`,
# ...), so substituting a bare region gives you a hostname that doesn't resolve.
# Copy the whole URI verbatim from your Supabase project's connection panel (take
# the transaction-pooler / port-6543 string) and substitute only your password.
# A host that doesn't resolve is NOT the demo case: it lands on the error page,
# which is correct but tells you nothing about the typo.

# Port 6543 is the transaction pooler, not the direct 5432 connection. The pooler
# does not support prepared statements, so the client in lib/db.ts is created with
# `prepare: false`, which turns them off. Leave prepared statements ON (postgres.js
# enables them by default) and queries fail intermittently.
#
# If your password contains any of : / ? # [ ] @ % it must be percent-encoded here.
#
# Apply the schema with `npm run db:migrate` (it applies supabase/migrations/*.sql
# in order and is safe to re-run), then optionally `npm run seed` for sample data.

# Leave DATABASE_URL unset — or skip .env.local entirely — and the dashboard
# renders the bundled demo dataset instead of live data. Any *other* connection
# problem surfaces as an error page: demo numbers are never shown as if real.
